\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces (a) Converter loss mechanisms evaluated by \textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:pcond}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}}--\textup  {\hbox {\mathsurround \z@ \normalfont  (\ignorespaces \ref  {eq:pcoss}\unskip \@@italiccorr )}} for one cell, showing the dominance of low-side conduction at a duty cycle of $0.0625$. (b) Electronics-scope loss breakdown for the final pair, separating converter losses from package losses. (c) Segment current and the resulting vertical interconnect loss: the same through-substrate field dissipates essentially nothing carrying 48\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V and $0.991$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}W carrying 1\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}V, which is why the interconnect is not common mode across architectures.}}{5}{figure.3}}
\newlabel{fig:loss}{{3}{5}{(a) Converter loss mechanisms evaluated by \eqref {eq:pcond}--\eqref {eq:pcoss} for one cell, showing the dominance of low-side conduction at a duty cycle of $0.0625$. (b) Electronics-scope loss breakdown for the final pair, separating converter losses from package losses. (c) Segment current and the resulting vertical interconnect loss: the same through-substrate field dissipates essentially nothing carrying 48\,V and $0.991$\,W carrying 1\,V, which is why the interconnect is not common mode across architectures}{figure.3}{}}
