\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Physical realizability of the vertical interconnect. (a) Conductor-plane sequence for the switch-node connection: three material layers separate the terminal faces, but the interlayer redistribution layers are capture planes, so one build-up via level is required. (b) Component tier order before and after the repartition; exchanging the low-side switches with the flying capacitors leaves the component set, per-tier areas and stack height unchanged. (c) Worst via aspect ratio per vertical net at a source-bounded microvia pitch: the $606$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\textmu m crossing required by the reference order is eliminated and the worst aspect ratio falls from $5.05$ to $0.833$.}}{6}{figure.4}}
\newlabel{fig:realize}{{4}{6}{Physical realizability of the vertical interconnect. (a) Conductor-plane sequence for the switch-node connection: three material layers separate the terminal faces, but the interlayer redistribution layers are capture planes, so one build-up via level is required. (b) Component tier order before and after the repartition; exchanging the low-side switches with the flying capacitors leaves the component set, per-tier areas and stack height unchanged. (c) Worst via aspect ratio per vertical net at a source-bounded microvia pitch: the $606$\,\textmu m crossing required by the reference order is eliminated and the worst aspect ratio falls from $5.05$ to $0.833$}{figure.4}{}}
