\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Package deformation and assembly state. (a), (b) Out-of-plane displacement of the die surface for the frozen pair, on one shared scale, from the converged thermoelastic solve of Section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:thermech}, drawn on the $30\times 30$ die-surface nodes of the global mesh; both surfaces are domed, and the embedded architecture carries the larger peak-to-valley. (c) Change in die warpage when the converter footprint is enlarged, evaluated for the free package and for five explicit board constructions. The sign of the interaction reverses between the free and the mounted state, so a mechanical conclusion obtained in one state cannot be quoted in the other.}}{12}{figure.6}}
\newlabel{fig:assembly}{{6}{12}{Package deformation and assembly state. (a), (b) Out-of-plane displacement of the die surface for the frozen pair, on one shared scale, from the converged thermoelastic solve of Section~\ref {sec:thermech}, drawn on the $30\times 30$ die-surface nodes of the global mesh; both surfaces are domed, and the embedded architecture carries the larger peak-to-valley. (c) Change in die warpage when the converter footprint is enlarged, evaluated for the free package and for five explicit board constructions. The sign of the interaction reverses between the free and the mounted state, so a mechanical conclusion obtained in one state cannot be quoted in the other}{figure.6}{}}
