\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Final cross-domain comparison on the frozen repartitioned pair. The backside architecture dissipates $2.37$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}W more and runs $8.14$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}K hotter, yet carries a $16.3$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\% lower corner-joint displacement driver $\Delta u_{\mathrm  {c}}$ (d) and an $18.4$\tmspace  +\thinmuskip {.1667em}\% lower stabilised local inelastic response $\Delta \varepsilon _{\mathrm  {in}}$ (e). The two are distinct quantities: (d) is a displacement in micrometres and drives the submodel, (e) is the dimensionless per-cycle response it returns. Warpage is peak-to-valley. Panel (f) reports the measured cost of one complete evaluation on 48 cores; the thermoelastic and local solder solves are entered once each, not once per fixed-point iteration. The ordinates of (a)--(e) are truncated, as the axis break marks.}}{13}{figure.8}}
\newlabel{fig:final}{{8}{13}{Final cross-domain comparison on the frozen repartitioned pair. The backside architecture dissipates $2.37$\,W more and runs $8.14$\,K hotter, yet carries a $16.3$\,\% lower corner-joint displacement driver $\du $ (d) and an $18.4$\,\% lower stabilised local inelastic response $\Delta \varepsilon _{\mathrm {in}}$ (e). The two are distinct quantities: (d) is a displacement in micrometres and drives the submodel, (e) is the dimensionless per-cycle response it returns. Warpage is peak-to-valley. Panel (f) reports the measured cost of one complete evaluation on 48 cores; the thermoelastic and local solder solves are entered once each, not once per fixed-point iteration. The ordinates of (a)--(e) are truncated, as the axis break marks}{figure.8}{}}
